From 138b562f114bc73723128a4e0f21e49a7bb8445c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 15:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(spec-decode): dead DSpark verification rows by expected
 tokens per ms

A fixed survival cut (SPARK3_DSPARK_DEAD_ROWS_TAU) cannot follow the
break-even survival of a verification row, which is the row's price times
the step's token rate. At one stream a live row reads about 5 ms of routed
experts and a dead row costs about 0.5 ms; at eight streams rows share
experts, a live row costs less and each token is worth less time.

With SPARK3_DSPARK_DEAD_ROWS=ratio, adaptive verification profiles the FULL
graph shapes a second time at startup, on real (routed) rows. The host
budget still comes from the padded-row profile, where a scheduled row costs
what a dead one does. Each step the host also stages, for every live-draft
count L within that budget, the step's cost: the draft, every scheduled row
as padding, and the real-row increment of the anchors plus L live drafts.
The draft combine ranks the batch's admitted drafts by this step's
survival and keeps live the L most likely to survive that maximizes
expected tokens per millisecond; the other rows become dead rows as with the
fixed cut. Each program ranks the whole batch (up to 64 requests), so no
kernel or host sync is added. Beyond the captured shapes there is no
real-row price and every scheduled draft stays live. Off by default; output
is unchanged because the target verifies every committed token.
